=======================================================================
 test_Poisson_2D harness - checks around the original 2D test deck
=======================================================================

WHAT THIS IS

  A test harness around test_Poisson_2D.lgi, the original test of the
  2D Poisson disk sampler in LaGriT. The deck meshes a unit square
  three times:

    output01_unit_square_uniform.inp   uniform h = 0.05
    output02_unit_square_var.inp       variable h, WIDTH_FACTOR 10
    output03_unit_square_var.inp       variable h, WIDTH_FACTOR 20

  The variable h field comes from user_h_field_att.mlgi:

      h(x) = max(h0, h0 * WIDTH_FACTOR * |x|)        h0 = 0.01

  fine along the centre line x = 0, coarsening toward the sides.

  The harness runs the deck UNCHANGED, then checks every mesh it
  wrote: that the points are inside the square and the mesh is valid,
  and it MEASURES spacing, how well the spacing follows the h field,
  and triangle quality.

QUICK START

  ./run_test_poisson_2d.sh  [path-to-lagrit]

  then open, in a web browser,

      logs/summary.html

  That one page has the results table, the deck, and for every output
  its full measurement report and a picture.

  Requires: LaGriT, Python 3 (standard library only), a POSIX shell.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
FILES
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  00README                  this file

  The test (not edited by the harness):
  test_Poisson_2D.lgi       the deck
  unit_square.poly          the polygon
  user_h_field_att.mlgi     builds the variable h field

  The harness:
  run_test_poisson_2d.sh    runs the deck once and checks each output
  check_test_poisson_2d.py  does the measuring and draws the pictures.
                            Its opening comment defines every measure
                            exactly.
  test_Poisson_2D.cases     says what each output is: its polygon, h0,
                            field and WIDTH_FACTOR. The deck itself
                            carries no such annotations.

  Written by a run (deleted first, so an old file can never pass as
  a new result):

  output0*.inp              the three meshes
  h_field_out.inp           the h field, dumped by the .mlgi
  logs/                     everything else, see OUTPUT below

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
IF YOU CHANGE THE DECK OR THE .mlgi
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  The deck: edit test_Poisson_2D.cases to match. One line per output
  file the deck dumps.

  The .mlgi: edit make_field() in check_test_poisson_2d.py to match.
  The checker cannot read the field the sampler used (the driver
  deletes it before returning), so it carries its own copy of the
  formula. The "field" row of the results compares that copy with
  h_field_out.inp on every run, so a mismatch is reported as a FAIL
  rather than silently measuring against the wrong field.

  Only the LAST variable run's h_field_out.inp survives, because
  every run dumps to the same file name, so only that case is checked
  against it directly.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Each row is PASS or FAIL. The exit status is nonzero on any FAIL.

  run       the deck ran: no crash, no ERROR, no WARNING
  output0*  the file was written, and the HARD checks pass:
              - every mesh point inside or on the square
              - no inverted triangles
              - no triangle naming a point that does not exist
  field     the checker's copy of h(x) matches h_field_out.inp

  Everything else in the table is MEASURED, NOT JUDGED. Thresholds
  have not been calibrated yet, so these are for comparing runs and
  spotting change:

    nodes     points in the mesh
    density   points / integral of 1/h^2 over the square. Near 0.7
              for a well filled sample, for uniform and variable h
              alike.
    int min   smallest spacing between INTERIOR points, as a fraction
              of h. Each pair is judged against the larger of its two
              target spacings: d / max(h_p, h_q).
    close     interior points with a neighbour closer than that
    bnd med   typical spacing between points on the square's edges.
              The sampler places boundary points closer than h by
              design, so they are measured separately.
    tracking  how evenly the spacing follows the h field: 1.0 means
              the spacing is the same multiple of h everywhere.
    ang min   smallest angle of any triangle, degrees
    poor%     share of triangles with smallest angle below 20 deg

  NOTE: the deck connects every point set, so spacing is measured on
  the FINISHED mesh, after the driver has smoothed it. It is close to,
  but not exactly, what the sampler produced.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PICTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Each output has a picture in three panels.

  LEFT     the mesh points (dark dots). A RED line joins two points
           closer than h (pairs where both are on the boundary are
           not marked). A MAGENTA ring marks a point outside the
           square; there should be none.

  MIDDLE   the mesh, each triangle coloured by its smallest angle:
           dark red < 10, red < 20, orange < 30, light green < 40,
           green >= 40 degrees. A BLACK triangle is inverted (a FAIL).

  RIGHT    top: each point's distance to its nearest neighbour,
           plotted against x, with h(x) drawn in red. Blue dots are
           interior points, orange are boundary points. If spacing
           follows the field, the blue dots hug the red curve all the
           way across.
           middle and bottom: histograms of interior spacing / h and
           of smallest triangle angle. The red dashed lines mark d = h
           and the 20 degree "poor" threshold.

OUTPUT

  logs/summary.html           EVERYTHING ON ONE PAGE
  logs/<output>.svg           one output's picture
  logs/<output>.check         its full measurement report
  logs/field_<output>.check   the field comparison
  logs/run.all                all LaGriT output from the run

